---
/**
 * /guides/items-to-keep - the "please don't sell that" guide.
 *
 * SEO target: 'maplestory classic never sell', 'what items to keep
 * maplestory beginner', 'maplestory quest items I sold', 'maplestory
 * classic etc items guide'. This is a beginner-panic query - a new
 * player just NPC'd 30 Leaves for pocket change and now Fully Bloomed
 * Bluebell wants 20 of them for a chair recipe. Google-arriving on
 * this page should immediately answer "how do I know before selling?"
 *
 * Structure: five categories of never-sell items, each with an
 * ItemRewardStrip showing the concrete sprites + names + why. The
 * items are pulled from real quest-demand counts in items.json
 * (see the audit query in ROADMAP Sprint 61 for the source SQL).
 */

import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import ItemRewardStrip from "../../components/ItemRewardStrip.astro";
---

<BaseLayout
	title="Items You Should Never Sell - Liven's Classic Maple Guide"
	description="The quest-required ETC items, unique story rewards, and rare event drops new MapleStory Classic players sell for pocket change and regret 20 levels later. Concrete item list with sprites and why-not-to-sell reasons."
	currentPath="/guides"
	theme="perion"
	decorations={false}
>
	<section class="section-heading">
		<div>
			<span class="eyebrow eyebrow--guide">Beginner Reference</span>
			<h1>Items You Should Never Sell</h1>
			<p class="lede">
				Every returning MapleStory Classic player has the same story: they
				NPC'd a stack of Leaves for 15 mesos each at Lv 10, then hit Lv 25,
				talked to an NPC, and learned that same stack was worth a scroll
				drop, an EXP quest chain, or a piece of crafted gear worth ten times
				more. This guide is the "how do I know before I sell?" answer.
			</p>
		</div>
	</section>

	<article class="prose">
		<h2 id="the-fundamental-rule">The fundamental rule</h2>
		<p>
			<strong>If an ETC item has a specific name and a sprite that isn't a
			generic pile of coins, keep at least 30 of it until Lv 40.</strong>
			Generic drops (Ores, generic Slime Bubbles used for meso value only)
			are fine to sell. Named ETC items with distinct sprites almost always
			back a quest, a crafting recipe, or a collect-100-turnin somewhere.
		</p>
		<p>
			The reason: quest EXP scales with the level of the mob it came from.
			A 20-Leaf turn-in you complete at Lv 12 gives massive EXP; the same
			turn-in at Lv 35 is trivial. Selling ETC drops early is throwing away
			the best EXP conversion in the game.
		</p>

		<h2 id="category-a-etc-quest-turnins">
			Category A: ETC drops with hidden quest demand
		</h2>
		<p>
			These are the top offenders - unassuming ETC items that back FOUR OR
			MORE separate quests each. New players see the low NPC sell price,
			right-click, and lose thousands of EXP worth of turn-ins. The
			datamine numbers below (quest count per item) come straight from
			<code>items.json</code>.
		</p>

		<ItemRewardStrip
			title="The seven-quest MVPs"
			lede="Each of these ETC drops is required by 5 or more separate quests. Keep 30-50 of each stockpiled from your first drop through Lv 40 at minimum."
			itemIds={[4000011, 4000009, 4000013, 4000033, 4000016, 4000019, 4000031]}
			notes={{
				4000011: "needed by 7 quests",
				4000009: "needed by 6 quests",
				4000013: "needed by 6 quests",
				4000033: "needed by 6 quests",
				4000016: "needed by 5 quests",
				4000019: "needed by 5 quests",
				4000031: "needed by 5 quests",
			}}
		/>

		<ItemRewardStrip
			title="High-demand secondary drops (4 quests each)"
			lede="Slightly lower quest count but the same rule: don't sell until you've cleared every quest in the origin region. Charm of the Undead in particular gates several story quests around Sleepywood."
			itemIds={[4000039, 4000020, 4000036, 4000017, 4000018, 4000032, 4000028, 4000034]}
			notes={{
				4000039: "5 quests",
				4000020: "4 quests, Undead line",
				4000036: "4 quests, Drakes+Sleepywood",
				4000017: "4 quests, Stirges",
				4000018: "4 quests, Jr. Necki",
				4000032: "4 quests, beach quests",
				4000028: "4 quests, Ghost Ship line",
				4000034: "4 quests, Cold Eye Sleepywood",
			}}
		/>

		<h3>How to check if an ETC drop backs a quest</h3>
		<p>
			Every item on this site has an auto-generated detail page. Any drop's
			"Required by quests" section will list every quest that needs it,
			with quest levels and regions. Two workflows:
		</p>
		<ul>
			<li>
				<strong>Reactive:</strong> right-click any ETC drop in your
				inventory, click the item name, then look it up at
				<a href="/items">the items catalog</a>. The detail page lists
				every quest that demands it.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>Proactive:</strong> when you enter a new region, glance
				at <a href="/quests">the quests catalog</a>, filter by that
				region, and mentally note the ETC drops each quest wants. Then
				you know to bank them from the first mob.
			</li>
		</ul>

		<h2 id="category-b-story-quest-uniques">
			Category B: Story-quest unique rewards
		</h2>
		<p>
			Items given by a specific one-time story quest are usually harder to
			re-obtain than any drop. Some are outright impossible to get twice.
			Read the flavor text before selling - if it references a specific
			NPC or event, it's probably a keepsake with meaning down the line.
		</p>

		<ItemRewardStrip
			title="Notable one-time earrings"
			lede="Given by early-game quest chains. Even if the stats look mediocre now, they slot into intermediate/greater scrolls later that require the base earring. Bank them, don't NPC them."
			itemIds={[1032002, 1032012, 1032013, 1032015, 1032021, 1032022]}
			notes={{
				1032002: "Weighted - early quest reward",
				1032012: "Pansy - Ellinia line",
				1032013: "Skull - Kerning line",
				1032015: "Leaf - Henesys line",
				1032021: "Henesys Citizenship exclusive",
				1032022: "Kerning Citizenship exclusive",
			}}
		/>

		<p>
			The earrings above are especially dangerous because Classic World
			introduced a whole earring scroll ecosystem in Closed Online Test 2
			(Intermediate + Greater STR/DEX/INT/LUK scrolls). Those scrolls need
			a base earring to slot onto. Selling any early earring means
			potentially having to grind or PQ to reacquire the base for
			scrolling later.
		</p>

		<h2 id="category-c-scrolls">
			Category C: Scrolls, all of them
		</h2>
		<p>
			If it starts with "Scroll for" and ends with a percentage or
			"Chaos" - keep it. Even the 10% ones. Even the ones for slots you
			don't have gear in yet. Scrolls have meso floors in the auction
			house that make them almost always more valuable to sell there than
			to NPC, and specific scrolls (White Scroll, Chaos Scroll, 60%
			Overall DEX, 60% Glove ATT) hold value for the entire lifetime of
			the server.
		</p>
		<ul>
			<li>
				<strong>NPC sell prices for scrolls are laughably low</strong> -
				usually 100-500 mesos, sometimes single-digit thousands for
				high-tier scrolls that trade for tens of millions in the AH.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>Even "useless" scrolls have a market.</strong> A 10%
				Overall DEX 60 that fails 90% of the time is still worth
				thousands to a Warrior with a rare pristine Green Bandana who
				needs the +2 DEX. Someone will always want it.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>Chaos Scrolls and White Scrolls are basically currency</strong> -
				bank every single one. Even one at Lv 30 becomes meaningful.
			</li>
		</ul>

		<h2 id="category-d-event-and-limited">
			Category D: Event drops and coupons
		</h2>
		<p>
			Anything with a name that doesn't match the current season or
			mentions a holiday you're not currently in the middle of - keep it.
			Classic World's event calendar rotates, and event-exclusive items
			(Frozen Tuna, Pumpkin Basket, Present Box, Snowman Piece) become
			completely unavailable outside their windows. Some legacy event
			items rose 100x in value on GMS old servers when the events came
			back or the items were needed for later crafting recipes.
		</p>
		<p>
			Rule of thumb: if a drop has a Christmas tree, a jack-o-lantern, an
			egg, a heart, or a distinctly seasonal color palette, don't sell it
			until you've verified via <a href="/items">the items catalog</a>
			that it isn't gated behind an event you're not currently in.
		</p>

		<h2 id="category-e-class-gated-gear">
			Category E: Class-gated gear from other jobs
		</h2>
		<p>
			If you're a Warrior and you loot a Wand, it's tempting to NPC it for
			meso. Don't. Wands and Staves are always in demand from new Mages,
			and even a plain unwashed Wand can trade for 20-50k early on. Same
			for Claws (Thieves), Bows (Bowmen), and off-class Overalls.
		</p>
		<p>
			Two safe places to route off-class drops instead of NPC'ing:
		</p>
		<ul>
			<li>
				<strong>Give it to a partymate</strong> - if you're PQing or
				grinding with a mixed party, offer off-class drops as loot ties.
				Builds reputation for the long tail.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>Store in a mule</strong> - all Classic World characters
				share meso storage but not gear storage. Create a Lv 1 mule in
				the town of the class you loot for, park the gear there, sell
				via the Auction House / whisper trades when the market's up.
			</li>
		</ul>

		<h2 id="the-cheat-sheet">The cheat sheet</h2>
		<p>Before hitting the NPC sell button, run this five-question filter:</p>
		<ol>
			<li>Does this item have a specific proper name (not generic)?</li>
			<li>Is this item in a category that starts with "Etc" or "Setup"?</li>
			<li>Is my level under 40?</li>
			<li>Have I ever seen this item's name in a quest dialog?</li>
			<li>
				When I look it up in
				<a href="/items">the items catalog</a>, does it have any entries
				in "Required by quests" or "Used as material"?
			</li>
		</ol>
		<p>
			Two or more "yes" answers - bank it. All five - definitely bank it
			and add a mental note to check the catalog properly when you're
			back in town. The mesos you'd earn NPC'ing 30 Leaves at 15 mesos
			each is 450 - the equivalent of one Red Potion. The EXP you'd get
			from the 7 quests those Leaves back is enough to move a Lv 12
			character to Lv 14.
		</p>

		<p class="closing">
			Next up: read <a href="/guides/consumables">the Consumables Guide</a>
			for the flip side - what you SHOULD be buying instead of hoarding
			meso, and where the potion market breaks efficiency below Lv 30.
		</p>
	</article>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
	.section-heading {
		max-width: 68ch;
		margin-bottom: 2rem;
	}
	.lede {
		font-size: 1.05rem;
		color: var(--text-body);
		margin-top: 0.5rem;
		line-height: 1.55;
	}

	.prose {
		max-width: 72ch;
		font-size: 1rem;
		line-height: 1.62;
		color: var(--text-body);
	}
	.prose h2 {
		margin-top: 2.2rem;
		margin-bottom: 0.5rem;
		color: var(--text-heading);
		font-size: 1.5rem;
		border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border-card);
		padding-bottom: 0.35rem;
	}
	.prose h3 {
		margin-top: 1.4rem;
		margin-bottom: 0.4rem;
		color: var(--text-heading);
		font-size: 1.15rem;
	}
	.prose p,
	.prose ul,
	.prose ol {
		margin-top: 0.7rem;
		margin-bottom: 0.7rem;
	}
	.prose li {
		margin-bottom: 0.35rem;
	}
	.prose code {
		font-family: var(--font-mono);
		font-size: 0.88em;
		background: var(--bg-cloud);
		padding: 0.1em 0.35em;
		border-radius: 0.25rem;
		border: 1px solid var(--border-card);
	}
	.prose a {
		color: var(--accent-primary);
		text-decoration: underline;
		text-decoration-thickness: 0.09em;
		text-underline-offset: 0.2em;
	}
	.prose strong {
		color: var(--text-heading);
	}
	.prose .closing {
		margin-top: 2.2rem;
		padding-top: 1rem;
		border-top: 1px solid var(--border-card);
		font-style: italic;
		color: var(--text-muted);
	}
</style>
